const e="2026-11-06",t=[{notes:[{meaning:"时间表；日程安排",pos:"n.",word:"timetable"},{meaning:"透露；公布",pos:"v.",word:"disclose"},{meaning:"造成（损害、痛苦等）",pos:"v.",word:"inflict"},{meaning:"静态点火测试（火箭固定在地面点火测试）",pos:"n.",word:"static-fire test"},{meaning:"固定；锚定",pos:"v.",word:"anchor"},{meaning:"点燃；引燃",pos:"v.",word:"ignite"},{meaning:"核实；验证",pos:"v.",word:"verify"},{meaning:"停止；使停顿",pos:"v.",word:"halt"},{meaning:"翻新；整修",pos:"n.",word:"refurbishment"},{meaning:"许可；批准",pos:"n.",word:"clearance"},{meaning:"适度的；不大的",pos:"adj.",word:"modest"},{meaning:"应对；处理（困难）",pos:"phr.",word:"contend with"},{meaning:"浸透；使饱和",pos:"v.",word:"saturate"},{meaning:"倾盆的；猛烈的（雨）",pos:"adj.",word:"torrential"},{meaning:"冗余；后备保障",pos:"n.",word:"redundancy"},{meaning:"持久的；经久的",pos:"adj.",word:"durable"}],passage:`Blue Origin is targeting the first or second week of January for the next launch of its New Glenn rocket, the company's first flight since a violent explosion during a ground test in May inflicted extensive damage on its launch complex at Cape Canaveral in Florida. The timetable, disclosed by chief executive Dave Limp on Thursday at the UP.Summit technology conference held at NASA's Kennedy Space Center, represents the clearest indication yet of the pace of recovery for a programme whose return to flight had previously been expected before the end of the year.

The setback dates to 28 May, when a static-fire test — a standard procedure in which a rocket's engines are ignited while the vehicle remains firmly anchored to the ground — ended in an explosion that caused extensive damage across Launch Complex 36. Static-fire tests are designed to verify engine performance and ground systems under realistic conditions without the risk of an actual launch; when they fail, however, the consequences can be as severe as a failure in flight, since the vehicle is fully fuelled and the surrounding infrastructure is exposed to the full force of the blast. In this instance, the damage was sufficiently severe to halt launch operations for months while repairs were carried out and safety systems were rebuilt.

According to Limp, the first-stage booster — the reusable section of the rocket that provides the thrust to lift the vehicle off the pad and is intended to return to Earth for refurbishment and reuse — is now ready for flight. The upper stage, which carries the payload into its final orbit after the booster has separated, is scheduled to undergo its own hot-fire test within the next week or two at NASA's Stennis Space Center in Mississippi, a facility long used for testing large rocket engines. Only once that test has been completed successfully can engineers give the final clearance for the two stages to be assembled and prepared for launch. Fully stacked, New Glenn measures 320 feet (about 98 metres) in height, placing it among the largest operational rockets in the world.

The revised timetable represents a modest delay rather than a fundamental setback. In recent months, both Limp and the company's founder, Jeff Bezos, had expressed hope that the next launch might take place before the end of the year. That ambition has now slipped into early January, a delay that illustrates the caution with which the industry treats any return to flight after an accident, when every system must be re-examined before it can be trusted again. The company has not disclosed a specific date, and much will depend on the outcome of the forthcoming hot-fire test.

The recovery effort has also had to contend with the forces of nature. Between Sunday, 1 November, and Tuesday, 3 November, the National Weather Service station at Melbourne Orlando International Airport reported that up to 10.5 inches (about 267 millimetres) of rain fell on parts of NASA's Kennedy Space Center, saturating ground that was already being prepared for the resumption of launch operations. Such extreme weather is a reminder of the additional challenges faced by spaceports on Florida's Atlantic coast, where hurricanes, thunderstorms and torrential rain are a routine hazard for engineers working to tight schedules.

The stakes for Blue Origin are considerable. New Glenn is the company's heavy-lift vehicle, designed to carry large satellites into orbit and, eventually, to support missions beyond Earth orbit, including the delivery of cargo and crew to the Moon. Its success matters not only to the company but to the wider space industry: a reliable second provider of heavy-lift launches would increase competition, reduce costs and provide redundancy in a market that currently depends heavily on a single dominant operator. Whether the January target is met, therefore, will be watched closely by customers, competitors and policymakers alike, as a test of whether Blue Origin can convert a damaging failure into a durable recovery.`,questions:[{answer:1,options:["Before the end of 2026","In the first or second week of January","In late February","After March, once repairs are finished"],q:"When is Blue Origin now targeting the next launch of its New Glenn rocket?"},{answer:1,options:["A hurricane striking the Florida coast","An explosion during a static-fire test on 28 May","A fire in the upper-stage factory","A collision during the rocket's first flight"],q:"What caused the extensive damage to Launch Complex 36?"},{answer:0,options:["A test in which the rocket's engines are ignited while the vehicle remains anchored to the ground","A computer simulation of a launch","A test flight with no payload on board","A test of the rocket's heat shield in a wind tunnel"],q:"According to the passage, what is a static-fire test?"},{answer:2,options:["The upper stage","The payload fairing","The first-stage booster","The launch tower"],q:"According to Dave Limp, which part of New Glenn is ready for flight?"},{answer:1,options:["At Launch Complex 36 in Florida","At NASA's Stennis Space Center in Mississippi","At the Kennedy Space Center in Florida","At Blue Origin's factory in Texas"],q:"Where will the upper-stage hot-fire test take place?"},{answer:1,options:["True","False","Not Given"],q:"The heavy rain that fell on the Kennedy Space Center in early November helped to speed up the recovery effort."},{answer:0,options:["True","False","Not Given"],q:"New Glenn measures 320 feet in height when fully assembled."},{answer:0,options:["Because it would increase competition, reduce costs and provide redundancy in heavy-lift launches","Because it would end NASA's dependence on all commercial rockets","Because it is the only rocket capable of reaching the Moon","Because its success would guarantee a launch before the end of the year"],q:"Why does the passage say that New Glenn's success matters beyond Blue Origin itself?"}],source:"Florida Today, 5 November 2026 (Blue Origin targeting early January for first New Glenn launch since explosion)",title:"Back to the Launch Pad: Blue Origin Prepares New Glenn's Return to Flight"},{notes:[{meaning:"显著的；明显的",pos:"adj.",word:"pronounced"},{meaning:"基线；基准期",pos:"n.",word:"baseline"},{meaning:"反复出现的；周期性的",pos:"adj.",word:"recurring"},{meaning:"脆弱的；易受伤害的",pos:"adj.",word:"vulnerable"},{meaning:"灌溉",pos:"n.",word:"irrigation"},{meaning:"限制；约束",pos:"v.",word:"constrain"},{meaning:"稀缺的；不足的",pos:"adj.",word:"scarce"},{meaning:"复合的；叠加放大的",pos:"adj.",word:"compounding"},{meaning:"韧性；适应恢复能力",pos:"n.",word:"resilience"},{meaning:"经受；承受",pos:"v.",word:"withstand"},{meaning:"足够的；适当的",pos:"adj.",word:"adequate"},{meaning:"不足的；不够的",pos:"adj.",word:"insufficient"},{meaning:"（事件）前夕；筹备期",pos:"n.",word:"run-up"},{meaning:"面对；正视（难题）",pos:"v.",word:"confront"},{meaning:"落后于；跟不上",pos:"phr.",word:"lag behind"}],passage:`The number of days with above-average temperatures around the world has more than doubled since the early 1980s, according to a new report from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD), which warns that rising exposure to extreme heat and drought is posing a growing threat to economies, agriculture and energy supplies. The Climate Action Monitor 2026, presented on 5 November at a session of the COP31 virtual pavilion, finds that the global number of such days has increased by 126%, with particularly pronounced increases in Latin America and the Caribbean, Sub-Saharan Africa, and the Middle East and North Africa, where the figure has more than tripled.

The findings are based on a comparison between the climate of recent years and the baseline period of 1981 to 2010. Between 2021 and 2025, populations in OECD countries experienced 35% more heat stress days than during that baseline — equivalent to an additional 17 days per year on average — and about half of the population in OECD countries is now exposed to hot days, defined as temperatures exceeding 35°C, every year. What was once an exceptional event, in other words, has become a recurring feature of daily life for hundreds of millions of people.

The consequences extend well beyond human discomfort. Cropland soil moisture across OECD member and partner countries was on average 2.6% lower in 2021–2025 than in the 1981–2010 baseline, a decline that raises the risk of reduced agricultural production and disruption to global supply chains. When soils dry out, crops become more vulnerable to failure, irrigation demands rise, and the cost of food can increase, with the burden falling most heavily on low-income households and on countries that already depend on imports to feed their populations.

Drought can also disrupt energy production in ways that are often overlooked. Low river flows and high water temperatures can constrain nuclear power generation, since many reactors depend on river water for cooling and must reduce output — or shut down entirely — when that water becomes too warm or too scarce. Reduced water availability can likewise affect hydropower output, cutting electricity supplies at precisely the moment when demand for cooling is at its highest. The result is a compounding risk: the same heat that drives up the need for air conditioning can simultaneously reduce the capacity to supply it.

With drought severity projected to increase by 2100, the report notes that businesses and governments are already taking steps to strengthen resilience — adapting infrastructure, diversifying water supplies and redesigning supply chains to withstand shocks that were once considered rare. Yet adaptation, the report implies, is a response to a problem whose root cause remains unaddressed: as long as global temperatures continue to rise, the extremes of today will become the norms of tomorrow, and the measures that are adequate now may prove insufficient within a generation. The burden is also unevenly distributed: outdoor workers, older people and those without access to cooling bear the greatest share of the risk, turning what might appear to be a purely environmental challenge into a question of public health and social equity.

The timing of the report is no coincidence. It was released in the run-up to the COP31 climate summit, which opens on 9 November in Antalya, Türkiye, where negotiators will confront the widening gap between the finance available for adaptation and the finance required. A separate United Nations report published this week found that developing countries need about $305 billion a year to adapt to climate change, while international public finance reached only around $28 billion in 2024. The OECD's data on heat and drought provide the evidence behind those figures: they show, in concrete terms, what a warming world already costs — and what it will cost if action continues to lag behind the pace of change.`,questions:[{answer:2,options:["By 35%","By 17 days per year","By 126%, more than doubling","By 2.6%"],q:"By how much has the global number of days with above-average temperatures increased since the early 1980s?"},{answer:1,options:["East Asia and Oceania only","Latin America and the Caribbean, Sub-Saharan Africa, and the Middle East and North Africa","Northern Europe and North America","Central Asia and the Pacific islands"],q:"In which regions has the number of above-average-temperature days more than tripled?"},{answer:0,options:["35% more, equivalent to about 17 extra days a year","126% more, equivalent to about 35 extra days a year","About half as many again","2.6% more"],q:"Compared with the 1981–2010 baseline, how many more heat stress days per year did people in OECD countries experience between 2021 and 2025?"},{answer:2,options:["It was 2.6% higher than the 1981–2010 baseline","It was unchanged from the baseline","It was on average 2.6% lower than the 1981–2010 baseline","It fell by 126% in OECD countries"],q:"What does the passage say about cropland soil moisture in 2021–2025?"},{answer:0,options:["True","False","Not Given"],q:"Nuclear power stations sometimes have to reduce output when the river water used for cooling becomes too warm."},{answer:1,options:["True","False","Not Given"],q:"Hydropower output increases during droughts because rivers flow faster."},{answer:1,options:["True","False","Not Given"],q:"The OECD report states that the adaptation measures already taken will be sufficient for the rest of the century."},{answer:0,options:["To give negotiators concrete evidence of what warming already costs as they discuss the adaptation finance gap","To announce that the COP31 summit had been cancelled","To replace the United Nations Adaptation Gap Report","To celebrate the completion of the COP30 climate talks"],q:"Why was the Climate Action Monitor 2026 released just before the COP31 summit?"}],source:"OECD, 5 November 2026 (Rising exposure to extreme heat and droughts poses growing risks to economies, OECD finds)",title:"A Hotter, Drier World: The OECD Warns of Rising Risks from Heat and Drought"}],a={date:e,newsReadings:t};export{e as date,a as default,t as newsReadings};
